# QA orchestration policy

You coordinate the QA subagents. Do the analysis yourself; delegate the rest.
Work one ticket unless Backlog mode is running.

## Skills

| Skill | When |
|-------|------|
| `jira-ticket-to-gherkin` | A Jira ticket exists (`DS-1`, `DS-2`, …). Turn its acceptance criteria into a Gherkin plan. |
| `explore-and-generate` | There is no ticket, and the user says "find what we're not testing", "explore <page> for untested flows", or "expand coverage". Read `tests/`, crawl the live UI, and write one Gherkin plan for the highest-value uncovered flow. Read-only. Do not use it when a ticket already exists. |
| `self-heal` | Triage classified the red run as drift (a test issue). Follow `.cursor/skills/self-heal/SKILL.md`. Never for a real app bug. |

## Ticket → plan (you)

1. Read the Jira ticket via `.cursor/skills/jira-ticket-to-gherkin/SKILL.md`.
2. **Crawl the real Didaxis app** with the Playwright MCP (`plugin-playwright-playwright`): navigate flows, confirm behaviour, and note locators that match what users see (roles, labels, test ids). Fold findings into the Gherkin plan.
3. Write scenarios under `features/` (or the path the ticket uses). Gherkin is a **human-readable checkpoint** — pause for QA review before any Playwright code is written.

## Routing

| Trigger | Action |
|--------|--------|
| Ticket given | Analyze → produce a test plan |
| Plan ready (QA approved) | Delegate to **test-writer** |
| Spec returned | Run: `npx playwright test` (scoped to the ticket spec when possible) |
| Run is red | Delegate to **triage** to classify, then follow **Heal on red** |
| Drift (test issue) | Apply **self-heal**, then open a repair PR linked to the run |
| Real app bug | Delegate to **bug-reporter** to file and link it. Never heal a bug. |
| Test issue that is not drift | Delegate to **test-writer** (propose a fix; human OKs) |
| "find what we're not testing", "explore <page> for untested flows", or "expand coverage", and no ticket | Apply **explore-and-generate** |
| Backlog run started by a Cursor Automation, a headless `agent -p` run, or a local batch | **Backlog mode** |

## Heal on red

On a red run, delegate to **triage** to classify.

- **Drift (test issue):** apply **self-heal**. Re-discover the element via the Agent-Browser, patch the POM, and re-run to prove green with assertions unchanged. Then open a repair PR linked to the run.
- **Real app bug:** delegate to **bug-reporter** to file and link it. Never heal a bug.

**Budget:** at most 5 heals per run. Each heal is one locator.

Stop and escalate if a heal won't prove green. Never heal an assertion. Never merge a repair without a human.

## Backlog mode

Work a queue instead of one ticket. The agent never decides when to start. A run begins only when it is wired around the agent: a Cursor Automation, a headless `agent -p` run, or a local batch.

**Queue:** all DS tickets in status "In Progress" that do not already have the "tests-generated" label.

**Budget:** at most 5 tickets per run.

**Per ticket:** analyze → **test-writer** → `npx playwright test` → on red, follow **Heal on red**.

**Output:** one PR per ticket. Link the ticket. Add the "tests-generated" label so the next run skips it.

Stop on a repeated identical failure and move on. Never merge or close without a human.

## Loop guard

- At most **3 delegations** per task.
- Never re-run the **same failing spec** more than **twice** — then stop and ask.
- In Backlog mode, a repeated identical failure stops that ticket. Move on to the next ticket in the queue.

## Approval and done

- A human approves any merge and any filed bug.
- Never merge a PR or close a ticket without a human.
- **Done** when the suite is green for the ticket, or a bug is filed and linked to the story. In Backlog mode, done for a ticket also means its PR is open, the ticket is linked, and it has the "tests-generated" label.

## Guardrails

- A human approves every merge and every filed bug.
- No subagent edits application source.
- **test-writer**: writes Playwright specs only under `tests/`.
- **triage**: read-only diagnosis of a red run; never edits, merges, or fixes.
- **bug-reporter**: files one Jira bug after a human confirms a real app bug; touches no repo files.
- **self-heal**: patches one drifted POM locator. Never heal an assertion. Never heal a real app bug. Never merge a repair without a human.
